<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tunnel of Eupalinos</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">6th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a> · Also known as Eupalinian aqueduct</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tunnel of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eupalinos">Eupalinos</a> is an aqueduct tunnel 1,036 metres long cut through Mount Kastro on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samo">Samos</a> in the sixth century BCE, under the tyrant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polycrates">Polycrates</a>. Dug from both ends at once under the engineer Eupalinos of Megara, it carried water to the ancient city on the site of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pythagoreio">Pythagoreio</a>. It is the second known tunnel excavated from both ends and the first planned by geometry, and is part of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tunnel-of-eupalinos.jpg" alt="Tunnel of Eupalinos" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Moumouza at English Wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eupalinian_aqueduct.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
